{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall surely return them to your brother.</b> The Torah commands that we be careful in returning a lost item to its owner. And from the doubling of 'returning' our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: even a hundred times — and all this is of the ways of kindness and mercy, to teach that we are all one people, worthy that we should have one Father, and that each should desire the benefit of his fellow and have compassion on his property. And whether the lost item is a living creature or not a living creature, he is obligated to return it to his domain — and this is what it says: ״וכן תעשה לחמורו״, which is a living creature and an unclean beast; ״וכן תעשה לשמלתו״, even though it is not considered like a living creature; ״וכן תעשה לכל אבדת אחיך״ — any other utensils, even though they are not considered like a garment — ״לא תוכל להתעלם״, that you should not return it to his hand. And this that it says ״לא תוכל להתעלם״ is not to be understood regarding the return of a lost item alone, but the same judgment applies to all the other particulars and all the benefits that are within a person's power to bring to his fellow, or to remove and push away harm from him — behold, he is obligated in all of them, in the manner of what the verse says (Leviticus 19:18): ״ואהבת לרעך כמוך״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״והתעלמת מהם״, and it is written ״לא תוכל להתעלם״ — how so? An elder, and it is not in keeping with his dignity — you may ignore; hence other people — you may not ignore." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״והתעלמת מהם״ — from the children of men; ״לא תוכל להתעלם״ — from the Holy One, blessed be He, for He is the one who knows all, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 29:23): ״ואנכי היודע ועד נאם ה׳״." "<b>And if</b> you gain further insight into the commandment of returning a lost item, you will find that it contains an allusion and an awakening to the matter of the revival of the dead: that the Holy One, blessed be He, returns a lost item to its owner at the end of days, when each one will return to his original portion and to the one with whom he first merited it, and then the latter will return a lost item to its owner, even though there are those who maintain that its owner is the latter — and there you will return to the time of revival. And this is what is written (Psalms 139:16): ״גלמי ראו עיניך״ — that is, the comprehending faculty of me, Your eyes saw; and ״על ספרך כלם יכתבו״ — and the days that were formed in them and for that one faculty in them; and it is the latter to which it shall return — to her first husband or to the latter, or she shall be divided among them as a candle is divided, or their dust shall be kneaded together with hers. And because it mentioned here in the matter of the lost item the expression נדחים, therefore it says ״וכן תעשה לכל אבדת אחיך אשר תאבד ממנו ומצאתה לא תוכל להתעלם״ — you are not permitted to ignore the Holy One, blessed be He, ״לבלתי ידח ממנו נדח״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "The passage contains dense, cryptic allusions to the resurrection of the dead and possibly to the restoration of soul-partnerships at the end of days; the Hebrew is textually difficult in places (e.g., והוא אחרון שתהיה חוזרת, ושם תשוב לזמן התחיה) and has been rendered hyper-literally without smoothing. The exact verse citation for the closing phrase לבלתי ידח ממנו נדח is not provided by the author."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:26.066023960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}